WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.311
Someone collapses.

00:00:01.311 --> 00:00:03.059
Your actions matter now.

00:00:03.059 --> 00:00:08.958
This RedScore mini training explains the first steps in a sudden adult
emergency.

00:00:08.958 --> 00:00:14.347
It provides an overview and does not replace a practical first-aid course.

00:00:15.447 --> 00:00:17.414
First, keep yourself safe.

00:00:17.414 --> 00:00:21.272
Look for dangers from traffic, electricity or fire.

00:00:21.272 --> 00:00:25.811
Speak loudly to the person and gently touch their shoulders.

00:00:25.811 --> 00:00:31.939
If they do not respond, shout for help and arrange an emergency call
immediately.

00:00:33.039 --> 00:00:36.513
Call one one two, or ask someone else to call.

00:00:36.513 --> 00:00:38.401
Put the phone on speaker.

00:00:38.401 --> 00:00:43.385
Give the exact location, answer questions and follow instructions.

00:00:43.385 --> 00:00:48.520
The dispatcher can help you check breathing and start resuscitation.

00:00:48.520 --> 00:00:51.465
Ask specific bystanders for assistance.

00:00:52.565 --> 00:00:56.848
Open the airway by tilting the head back and lifting the chin.

00:00:56.848 --> 00:01:00.717
Check for normal breathing for no more than ten seconds.

00:01:00.717 --> 00:01:04.310
Look for chest movement, listen and feel for breath.

00:01:04.310 --> 00:01:06.521
Gasping is not normal breathing.

00:01:06.521 --> 00:01:12.877
If breathing is normal, use the recovery position, keep monitoring and keep
the person warm.

00:01:12.877 --> 00:01:17.920
If breathing is absent or abnormal, start chest compressions immediately.

00:01:19.020 --> 00:01:22.186
Place the adult on their back on a firm surface.

00:01:22.186 --> 00:01:27.924
Put the heel of one hand on the lower half of the breastbone and the other
hand on top.

00:01:27.924 --> 00:01:31.486
Keep your arms straight and shoulders over your hands.

00:01:31.486 --> 00:01:37.884
Give one hundred to one hundred and twenty compressions per minute, five to
six centimetres deep.

00:01:37.884 --> 00:01:41.512
Allow the chest to recoil fully after each compression.

00:01:41.512 --> 00:01:44.150
Keep interruptions as short as possible.

00:01:45.250 --> 00:01:52.311
If you are willing and able to give rescue breaths, alternate thirty chest
compressions with two breaths.

00:01:52.311 --> 00:01:58.431
If you cannot or do not want to give breaths, continue chest compressions
without stopping.

00:01:58.431 --> 00:02:00.919
Follow the dispatcher’s instructions.

00:02:00.919 --> 00:02:02.936
Taking action is what matters.

00:02:04.036 --> 00:02:09.890
If an automated external defibrillator is available, ask another helper to
bring it.

00:02:09.890 --> 00:02:13.236
Do not leave the person just to look for an AED.

00:02:13.236 --> 00:02:16.093
Switch it on and follow its instructions.

00:02:16.093 --> 00:02:19.090
Attach the pads to the bare chest as shown.

00:02:19.090 --> 00:02:23.132
Nobody should touch the person during analysis or a shock.

00:02:23.132 --> 00:02:26.686
Then resume compressions immediately as instructed.

00:02:27.786 --> 00:02:36.357
Continue resuscitation until emergency professionals take over, or the
person begins breathing normally and shows signs of life.

00:02:36.357 --> 00:02:42.585
If other helpers are available, swap approximately every two minutes with
only a brief pause.

00:02:42.585 --> 00:02:46.067
If you can no longer continue, shout for assistance.

00:02:47.167 --> 00:02:48.506
Remember: Check.

00:02:48.506 --> 00:02:49.928
Call one one two.

00:02:49.928 --> 00:02:50.681
Compress.

00:02:50.681 --> 00:02:53.609
And use an AED if one is available.

00:02:53.609 --> 00:02:58.043
Practise these steps in a practical first-aid course.

00:02:58.043 --> 00:03:01.724
This video is not an attendance certificate.

00:03:01.724 --> 00:03:06.409
